Here is my specification.
I have a list of elements. Generate Kotlin code to do the following.

Generate all partitions of these elements, from smallest partition (all elements in one set) to largest (all elements in separate sets). For example, say I have 3 elements called a, b, c; we should get:
partition of size 1: abc
partitions of size 2: ab c, a bc, ac b
partitions of size 3: a b c
It doesn't matter how we order within each partition size, but it matters that all partitions of size 2 come before all those of size 3.

Each partition contains some blocks.
For each partition, generate a subsequence of outputs; these will all be flatmapped into one big sequence.
The subsequence is as follows.
We have some partition containing m partitions. We also have some number n.
We can eiher set aside one of the blocks as a "labelled" blocks, or not.
For the remaining blocks (all of them if we chose not to have a labelled set), generate all possible ways of INJECTIVELY assigning SOME SUBSET of the numbers 0 to n to SOME SUBSET of them. That is, not all the blocks need be assigned to a number, and not all numbers up to n need to be assigned to a block.
^ it's the same as picking some number k leq n, all ways of nPk then remaining sets are just given numbers over n
All subsequences for one partition must be generated before any following partition is generated?
Write a detailed plan for this before writing Kotlin code. There is some combinatorics stuff going on here, describe your reasoning and how you will do this efficiently, WITHOUT ENUMERATING DUPLICATES.

This entire sequence should be lazy.

Review the two responses for efficiency, correctness, and cleanliness of code.





Write examples of OCaml expressions that might appear in a reasonable program such that ALL functions and values in the OCaml Stdlib (read it here: https://ocaml.org/manual/5.4/api/Stdlib.html) are used at least 5 times in some example.
ALL programs must be in PREFIX notation. You may use let statements, match statements, and function definitions, but keep them simple and not too nested. The ideal is just top-level expressions with no bindings.
You may use literals such as 0, true, and "hello world", but try to use as few of these as possible. That is, if you use 0 and 1, try to just reuse those in all examples rather than pulling out other arbitrary numbers such as 42.
You may not use any functions that are *not* included in the Stdlib.
EXCLUDE the Debugging and format strings sections of the Stdlib.
You should also INCLUDE ONLY the following modules in the Stdlib: Bool, List, Int, Pair.
As best you can, separate your responses by what they are mainly examples for. For insteance, you might have an example which contains function composition but mostly deals with I/O.


