No, you're not allowed to use anything that isn't in the list I gave. For example, you created the list [6,7], but that's not allowed. If you want a list of ints, you can construct one from 0, IL, and/or some other functions like (++) and (:). Write everything in prefix notation

No, you're not allowed to use lambdas either. You can only do function applications using the literals I've given










If they are runtime-generated temporary executables, place them in a dedicated application-controlled directory, such as:

A subdirectory under your project root (e.g., generated/ or runtime-tools/)

The system temporary directory via Files.createTempDirectory()

A configurable output directory defined in your application settings

Do not write runtime-generated executables into src/, build/, or out/ unless they are part of the build process. The build directory may be cleaned automatically, and out is IDE-specific and not reliable for production workflows.

For command-line programs meant to persist across runs, define an explicit output directory and manage it intentionally rather than relying on build tool directories.

In kotlin projects, where should I place temporary files generated at runtime? They contain small scripts that I run as a subroutine. It should be idiomatic but as simple as possible. I want it to be localized to the project root directory, not lying around in my system folders where I have to leave the directory to manually delete it
